/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An open system lets mass cross its boundary.
   - ṁ is the mass flow rate (kg/s); ρ the density (kg/m³); A the flow area (m²); V the mean velocity (m/s); v = 1/ρ the specific volume (m³/kg).
   - The steady flow energy equation (SFEE) rests on four assumptions:
   - Per kilogram of fluid, energy in equals energy out:
   - h is the specific enthalpy (kJ/kg); V the velocity (m/s); z the height above a datum (m); g = 9.81 m/s²; q the heat added (kJ/kg, negative if lost); w the shaft work delivered (kJ/kg, negative if work is put in).
   - The 2000 and 1000 are unit fixes.
   - Why enthalpy?
   - Multiply every term by ṁ for the rate form in kW: Q̇ = ṁq is the heat transfer rate and Ẇ = ṁw the shaft power.
   - Every device is the same equation with some terms removed.
   - In a throttle the pressure drops but h stays the same.
*/
